; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Peer two Peer — DNS zone fragment.
;
; Paste into the zone for the domain you control, replace every <angle bracket>,
; and change nothing else. This file is a fragment, not a zone: it has no SOA
; and no NS records, because those belong to whoever runs the nameservers and
; overwriting them is how a domain goes dark.
;
; Nothing here is required to reach the network. It is a convenience layer over
; a content address that already works without any of it — see README.md, "why
; the name is not the network". Everything below can be revoked by a registrar
; and the network is unaffected.
;
; Regenerate the values with:   node ops/domain/name.mjs
; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

; The zone this fragment is written for. Every unqualified name below is
; relative to it, so setting this wrongly silently misfiles every record.
$ORIGIN <example.org>.

; One hour. Short on purpose: the DNSLink record changes at every publication,
; and a day-long TTL means a day of readers served the previous site by their
; own resolver's cache, with nothing anyone can do about it from here.
$TTL 3600

; ── 1. DNSLink: the record that points the name at the content ───────────────
;
; A resolver asks for TXT at _dnslink.<domain>; a DNSLink-aware gateway reads
; the answer and serves that content address. This one line is the whole of the
; mechanism.
;
; Publish the /ipfs/ form OR the /ipns/ form, never both: two _dnslink values on
; one name means the resolver picks, and it may pick differently on different
; days for different readers.
;
; /ipfs/ — exact bytes, immutable. What a reader gets is what the operator meant
; on the day they set it. It goes stale at the next publication, and updating it
; is a deliberate DNS edit, which is the point: nothing changes under a reader
; without somebody changing it.
_dnslink        IN  TXT   "dnslink=/ipfs/<cid>"

; /ipns/ — the mutable pointer, signed by the genesis producer key. Set this
; instead if you want the domain to follow the network forward without a DNS
; edit at every publication. The cost is that the key holder can change what
; this domain serves without touching DNS, and that IPNS records expire: stop
; republishing and the name goes quiet while the CID underneath still works.
;_dnslink       IN  TXT   "dnslink=/ipns/<ipns-name>"

; ── 2. The gateway: how a browser that speaks only HTTP gets there ───────────
;
; A gateway is a courtesy someone else runs. It is not part of the network, it
; can disappear without notice, and it can serve you something other than what
; the CID says — which is exactly why the CID is checkable and why README.md
; tells readers how to check it. Pick a gateway that resolves DNSLink from the
; Host header; if it does not, this CNAME serves that gateway's landing page.
;
; Candidates, none of them endorsements and all of them worth testing before
; you publish: dweb.link, ipfs.io, w3s.link, 4everland.io, or your own kubo with
; `Gateway.PublicGateways` configured for this domain.
www             IN  CNAME <gateway.example>.

; The apex (the bare domain) cannot hold a CNAME — RFC 1034 forbids a CNAME
; beside the SOA and NS records that must live there. Two ways out, both from
; the registrar rather than from this file:
;   * an ALIAS/ANAME record, if your DNS provider offers one (flattened CNAME);
;   * an A/AAAA record at the gateway's addresses, which breaks silently when
;     the gateway renumbers — prefer ALIAS, and prefer neither: send people to
;     www, or to the CID.
;<example.org>. IN  ALIAS <gateway.example>.

; ── 3. CAA: who is allowed to issue a certificate for this name ──────────────
;
; Without a CAA record, every public CA in the world may issue for this domain,
; and a name is only as good as the weakest CA that will mint a certificate for
; it. These three lines cut that set to one.
;
; `issue` — the only CA permitted to issue ordinary certificates. Set it to the
; one your gateway actually uses; a CAA naming a CA you do not use is an outage
; at the next renewal, not security.
@               IN  CAA   0 issue "letsencrypt.org"

; `issuewild` with an empty value forbids wildcard certificates outright. A
; wildcard is one stolen key away from being every subdomain at once, and this
; zone has no use for one.
@               IN  CAA   0 issuewild ";"

; `iodef` — where a CA reports a request it refused. Optional, and the only line
; here that carries a human contact; leave it out rather than publish an address
; you do not read, because an unread report is worse than none.
@               IN  CAA   0 iodef "mailto:<security@example.org>"

; ── 4. Nothing else ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; No MX, no SPF, no DKIM, no DMARC records are included, because this network
; sends no mail and receives none. If the domain is also a mail domain, keep
; whatever it already has — but publish SPF/DMARC that REFUSE mail if it is not,
; since a domain with no mail policy is a domain anyone may forge:
;@              IN  TXT   "v=spf1 -all"
;_dmarc         IN  TXT   "v=DMARC1; p=reject; rua=mailto:<security@example.org>"

; Verify the record actually landed, from a machine that is not yours:
;   dig +short TXT _dnslink.<example.org> @1.1.1.1
;   curl -sI https://<example.org>/ | grep -i x-ipfs-path
